                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "073b7a30-2d45-318d-af1a-7e5809974c43",
                                        "entry_id": "0819210c-6efd-38ff-b0d5-b7b0a64b4695",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Composed of, or relating to, only one sex."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fd7cdd06-8d23-4114-8b2b-45ff8b873c60",
                                                "sense_id": "073b7a30-2d45-318d-af1a-7e5809974c43",
                                                "text": "The environment was composed of the students and servants who worked in the schools of Puebla, masculine and monosexual spaces that offered a favorable ambiance for the practice of the nefarious sin. Bear in mind that in pre-industrial societies it was not easy for an individual to escape the promiscuity of everyday life, hiding his acts from his relatives[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "922e15e8-5fe6-45d7-9b0e-028ea1d798e9",
                                                "sense_id": "073b7a30-2d45-318d-af1a-7e5809974c43",
                                                "text": "And it was biopolitical in that, by assigning Barbin a \"true\" and \"definite\" identity as a man, the magistrates maintained sexual dimorphism as the law of populations, even or perhaps particularly in \"monosexual\" spaces such as the convent.Thus Barbin, as a person assigned to the category of maleness, could no longer belong to the world of the convent."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "06fe6c78-871c-4d73-9178-cfa137f93da5",
                                                "sense_id": "073b7a30-2d45-318d-af1a-7e5809974c43",
                                                "text": "On the other hand, he did not go to a monosexual [all-boys] school as I did, as Isherwood and his kind did."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "728a690e-6028-32a5-aaf1-9de3b7c477cc",
                                        "entry_id": "0819210c-6efd-38ff-b0d5-b7b0a64b4695",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Sexually attracted to members of only one sex or gender."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2d296a20-c441-4ed5-ba18-f20c0c819411",
                                                "sense_id": "728a690e-6028-32a5-aaf1-9de3b7c477cc",
                                                "text": "How are we to understand the erasure of bisexuality in some of the fundamental works of queer deconstructive theory? […] a reliance on monosexual gay/lesbian historiography […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d456cd59-ca9c-4d03-a4fa-93b0ba8db5b6",
                                                "sense_id": "728a690e-6028-32a5-aaf1-9de3b7c477cc",
                                                "text": "[…] lesbian and gay people (i.e., monosexual minorities) regardless of sex and gender identities often face significant health disparities in relation to heterosexual people, that bisexual/pansexual/queer/fluid people (i.e. non-monosexual people) face significant disparities in relation to some lesbian/gay monosexual people and to monosexual heterosexual people in particular, and that asexual people (i.e., non-monosexual and non-sexual identified people) often experience significant disparities in relation to all of these[…]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "monosexual",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2bde8f5c-ea85-3082-9ea8-1d82c5e53564",
                                        "entry_id": "a9d7963b-fbe4-3bea-8b72-6a486d6a38ed",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A monosexual person."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9a02e8e6-44c2-4772-8147-0ff8a8c08302",
                                                "sense_id": "2bde8f5c-ea85-3082-9ea8-1d82c5e53564",
                                                "text": "Letitia, I fear, is a monosexual. Only men arouse her."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a56ab221-84e9-4460-accc-e8be693208ee",
                                                "sense_id": "2bde8f5c-ea85-3082-9ea8-1d82c5e53564",
                                                "text": "There are several other reasons for underestimating the number of lesbians. One, an effeminate male is usually associated with homosexuality, whether or not he is a monosexual. But masculine women are not usually defined as homosexual. Therefore, the defining of visual characteristics varies for men and women."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "de2e5754-2cfe-4a74-b379-6c60bf33c734",
                                                "sense_id": "2bde8f5c-ea85-3082-9ea8-1d82c5e53564",
                                                "text": "While monosexuals (gay men, heterosexuals, and lesbians) tend to describe being attracted to people of only one gender, bisexuals tend to disregard gender when assessing attractions to others."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
